          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} simple graphs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is a
            %strong graph minor
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            if it is isomorphic to a graph obtained by applying a sequence of operations of the following sort, starting from
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %ul
            %li
              %p Removing edges;
            %li
              %p Removing isolated points;
            %li
              %p Contracting edges, where contracting an edge means removing it and identifying its endpoints.
          %p Graph minors can be viewed as certain types of subquotients in an appropriate category of graphs; this article describes which subquotients those are in categorical language.
          .num_remark
            %h6#disclaimer Disclaimer
            %p To be honest, it’s not really clear how a categorical POV (or at least the POV presented here) can usefully inform or guide or clarify graph theory. For the time being, we are just collecting some notes. No deep theorems of graph theory are proved here. The main observation is simply that graphs form a category with very good properties, and there may be potential of putting those properties to effective use some day.
          %h2#categorical_pov Categorical POV
          %p
            We take the point of view that a
            %em simple graph
            (consisting of a set of vertices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and a collection of two-element subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            called “edges”) carries the same information as a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            equipped with a reflexive symmetric relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            where for given vertices
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x, y\}
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            iff both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y) \in E
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \neq y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(x, y)
            to mean
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x, y) \in E
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Since edges are
              = succeed "-element" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              equipped with a symmetric
              %em irreflexive
              relation
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(x, x)
              is false for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, E) \to (W, F)
              to be functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: V \to W
              which preserve those relations. Indeed, we could never have both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(x, y)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x) = f(y)
              since irreflexivity of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              would render
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(f(x), f(y))
              false. Thus, requiring reflexivity is the more flexible option.
          %p
            Notice that contraction of edges yields a quotient in this category. For example, if we want to contract a collection of edges by identifying certain points along an equivalence relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q: V \to V/R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo ×
                      %mi q
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi R
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi R
                  %mo ;
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \hookrightarrow V \times V \stackrel{q \times q}{\to} V/R \times V/R;
          %p
            notice this image is a reflexive symmetric relation on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V/R
          %p
            Thus, we let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SimpGph
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SimpGph
            denote the category of sets equipped with a reflexive symmetric relation. This category is convenient in many respects (for details see
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+simple+graphs"} category of simple graphs
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SimpGph
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SimpGph
              has the following properties.
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  It is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+quasitopos"} Grothendieck quasitopos
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi SimpGph
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SimpGph^{op}
                  are regular categories.
              %li
                %p
                  It is an
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
              %li
                %p
                  The forgetful functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo =
                        %mi hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %mi SimpGph
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \hom(1, -): SimpGph \to Set
                  is faithful and exhibits
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi SimpGph
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SimpGph
                  as a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+concrete+category"} topological concrete category
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi SimpGph
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SimpGph
                  is
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %em cohesive
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo ∇
                        %mo :
                        %mi Set
                        %mo →
                        %mi SimpGph
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta \dashv \Gamma \dashv \nabla: Set \to SimpGph
                  has a further left adjoint
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Π
                          %mo ⊣
                          %mi Δ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi \dashv \Delta
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                        %mo :
                        %mi SimpGph
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi: SimpGph \to Set
                  preserves products.
          %p
            Of course
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            assigns to a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the discrete graph on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (the minimal reflexive relation
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi X
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_X \hookrightarrow X \times X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            the codiscrete graph (the maximal relation
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{X \times X}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(G)
            is the set of connected components of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma: 1 \to \Delta \Pi
            denote the unit of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi \dashv \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi Δ
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma G: G \to \Delta \Pi (G)
            takes a vertex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in G
            to the connected component to which it belongs. Notice that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma G
            is a regular epi.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %em contraction
              map between simple graphs is a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi: G \to G'
              that arises by pushing out a map of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma K
              along some map
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi K
                      %mo →
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: K \to G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi γ
                                %mi K
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mi po
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi π
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Π
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi K
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                            %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    K &amp; \stackrel{f}{\to} &amp; G \\
                    \\mathllap{\gamma K} \downarrow &amp; po &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{\pi} \\
                    \\Pi(K) &amp; \to &amp; G'
                    }
            %p
              is a pushout square. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to G'
              is a contraction map, we also say
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G'
              is a
              %em contraction
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            It is immediate that a contraction map is a regular epi, since every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma K
            is regular epi.
          #composition.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p Contraction maps are closed under composition.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is a
              %em minor
              of a graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              if it arises as a subobject of some contraction of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi contraction
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi H
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↣
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                            %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; &amp; G \\
                    \&amp; &amp; \downarrow \mathrlap{contraction} \\
                    H &amp; \rightarrowtail &amp; G'
                    }
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p The subquotient relation is reflexive and transitive.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p Reflexivity is clear. For transitivity, we compose subquotients by taking a pushout square as follows.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↠
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↠
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                            %mo ″
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi mono
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi mono
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi H
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↠
                          %mtd
                            %mi H
                            %mo ′
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi mono
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    G &amp; \twoheadrightarrow &amp; G' &amp; \twoheadrightarrow &amp; G'' \\
                    \&amp; &amp;  ^\mathllap{mono} \uparrow &amp; &amp; \uparrow^\mathrlap{mono} \\
                    \&amp; &amp; H &amp; \twoheadrightarrow &amp; H' \\
                    \&amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \uparrow^\mathrlap{mono} \\
                    \&amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; J
                    }
            %p
              where we use the simple fact that the pushout of a mono along an arrow in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SimpGph
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SimpGph
              is a mono (because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vert
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vert
              reflects monos and preserves monos and pushouts, plus the fact that the pushout of a mono in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              is a mono).
          %p
            For finite graphs, the subquotient relation is also antisymmetric (if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            are minors of one another, then they are isomorphic). Indeed, if either the arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ↠
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \twoheadrightarrow G'
            or the arrow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \hookrightarrow G'
            is not an isomorphism, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            has strictly fewer edges and vertices than
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p (To be expanded, eventually. Hopefully one can develop a categorical story about graph minors in particular.)
          %h2#minorclosed_families Minor-closed families
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The collection of
                %span.newWikiWord
                  forests
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/forests"} ?
                is closed under the graph minor relation.
            %li
              %p
                The collection of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+graphs"} planar graphs
                is closed under the graph minor relation.
          %h2#graph_minor_theorem Graph minor theorem
          %p
            This celebrated result of Robertson and Seymour says that no collection of finite graphs, ordered by the graph minor relation, has an infinite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antichain"} antichain
          %p
            As a corollary, any class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of finite graphs which is closed under minors admits a
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong forbidden minor characterization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            in the poset of finite graphs has finitely many minimal members. Then,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            belongs to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            iff none of these minimal members occurs as a minor of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            For example, the class of forests is precisely the class of finite graphs which do not have a triangle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_3
            as a minor. The class of planar graphs is precisely the class of finite graphs which do not have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_5
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
